T1  - Cerebral microangiopathy in patients with non-insulin-dependent diabetes mellitus.
A1  - Petrica, Ligia
A1  - Petrica, Maxim
A1  - Munteanu, Mircea
A1  - Vlad, Adrian
A1  - Bob, Flaviu
A1  - Gluhovschi, Cristina
A1  - Gluhovschi, Gheorghe
A1  - Jianu, Catalin
A1  - Schiller, Adalbert
A1  - Velciov, Silvia
A1  - Trandafirescu, Virginia
A1  - Bozdog, Gheorghe
Y1  - 2007//
N2  - INTRODUCTION: The aim of the study was to evaluate cerebral microangiopathy in type 2 noninsulin- dependent diabetes mellitus (NIDDM) patients and to establish potentially conducive factors., MATERIALS AND METHODS: A group of 34 patients with NIDDM and 31 gender- and agematched normal controls (NC) were assessed by extracranial Doppler ultrasound, in order to evaluate the pulsatility index (PI) and the resistance index (RI) in the internal carotid arteries (ICAs); transcranial Doppler was utilised to assess the same parameters in the middle cerebral arteries (MCAs). All patients underwent screening for favouring factors for cerebral vascular remodelling., RESULTS: Of the 34 NIDDM patients, 21 patients (61.76%) (subgroup A) presented with microangiopathic complications [of these, 19 patients (90.46%) had diabetic nephropathy (DN)] versus 13 NIDDM patients (38.24%) (subgroup B) without complications. In subgroup A, 16 patients (76.19%) had PI >1 and RI >0.7 in the ICAs and MCAs (changes consistent with cerebral microangiopathy) versus 5 patients (35.46%) in subgroup B, and no modifications in NC. Of the 19 patients with DN, 14 patients (73.68 %) had impaired haemodynamic indices. Univariate regression analysis showed the following risk factors for the cerebral haemodynamics changes: fibrinogen (F) (OR = 3.11), C-reactive protein (CRP) (OR = 2.40), duration of DM (OR = 2.40), proteinuria (OR = 1.80), serum creatinine (OR = 1.66). Multivariate regression analysis showed as predictors for impaired haemodynamic indices: duration of DM (HR =1.70), proteinuria (HR = 1.70). The haemodynamic indices in the ICAs correlated with duration of DM (r = 0.87, P <0.0001), F (r = 0.86; P <0.0001), CRP (r = 0.80; P <0.0001); in the MCAs with the duration of DM (r = 0.66, P <0.0001), F (r = 0.38; P <0.0001), CRP (r = 0.88; P <0.0001)., CONCLUSION: Cerebral microangiopathy has a high prevalence in NIDDM patients. These cerebral vascular changes correlate with the duration of DM, parameters of inflammation, and proteinuria.
