T1  - Macrophage migration inhibitory factor is a therapeutic target in treatment of non-insulin-dependent diabetes mellitus.
DO  - https://dx.doi.org/10.1096/fj.09-147066
A1  - Sanchez-Zamora, Yuriko
A1  - Terrazas, Luis I
A1  - Vilches-Flores, Alonso
A1  - Leal, Emmanuel
A1  - Juarez, Imelda
A1  - Whitacre, Caroline
A1  - Kithcart, Aaron
A1  - Pruitt, James
A1  - Sielecki, Thais
A1  - Satoskar, Abhay R
A1  - Rodriguez-Sosa, Miriam
Y1  - 2010//
Y2  - 20100304//
N2  - Macrophage migration inhibitory factor (MIF) is a proinflammatory cytokine involved in the pathogenesis of a variety of autoimmune inflammatory diseases. Here, we investigated the role of MIF in the pathogenesis of non-insulin-dependent diabetes mellitus (NIDDM) using MIF(-/-) mice and a mouse model of streptozotocin (STZ)-induced NIDDM. Following single injection of STZ, MIF(+/+) BALB/c mice showed a significant increase in blood glucose levels, developed polyuria, and succumbed to disease. In contrast, no such increase in blood glucose was observed in MIF(-/-) BALB/c mice treated with STZ. These mice produced significantly less inflammatory cytokines and resistin as compared with MIF(+/+) mice and failed to develop clinical disease. Finally, oral administration of a small-molecule MIF antagonist, CPSI-1306, to outbred ICR mice following induction of NIDDM significantly lowered blood glucose levels in the majority of animals, which was also associated with a significant reduction in the levels of the proinflammatory cytokines IL-6 and TNF-alpha in the sera. Taken together, these results demonstrate that MIF is involved in the pathogenesis of NIDDM and is a therapeutic target to treat this disease.
