T1  - Plasma protein binding of lidocaine and warfarin in insulin-dependent and non-insulin-dependent diabetes mellitus.
DO  - https://dx.doi.org/10.2165/00003088-199324020-00007
A1  - O'Byrne, S
A1  - Barry, M G
A1  - Collins, W C
A1  - O'Connor, P
A1  - Cullen, M J
A1  - Feely, J
Y1  - 1993//
N2  - We examined the plasma protein binding of an acidic drug (warfarin bound to albumin) and a basic drug [lidocaine (lignocaine) bound to alpha 1-acid glycoprotein] in 15 patients with insulin-dependent diabetes mellitus (IDDM) and 15 matched controls. We also examined protein binding of warfarin and lidocaine in 30 patients with non-insulin-dependent diabetes (NIDDM) and 25 controls. Compared with control, the binding of both warfarin (98.81 +/- 0.02 vs 98.57 +/- 0.03%, mean +/- SEM) and of lidocaine (69 +/- 2 vs 58 +/- 2%) was significantly reduced in IDDM. This group had lower concentrations of both albumin and alpha 1-acid glycoprotein (AAG), achieving statistical significance vs control for albumin only. In the patients with NIDDM, who had a similar level of glycosylated haemoglobin, while there was no significant difference in the binding of lidocaine there was a significant increase in warfarin binding compared with the control population (99.01 +/- 0.03 vs 98.82 +/- 0.04%). This study suggests that binding of both acidic and basic drugs is altered in both IDDM and NIDDM.
